#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045486 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/hard #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046227 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematics for Machine Learning" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Mathematics for Machine Learning course provides a comprehensive foundation of the essential mathematical tools required to study modern machine learning.\nThis course is divided into three main categories: linear algebra, multivariable calculus, and probability & statistics. The linear algebra section covers crucial machine learning fundamentals such as matrices, vector spaces, diagonalization, projections, singular value decomposition, and regression, as well as dimensionality reduction techniques such as principal component analysis. The multivariable calculus section develops the tools needed for optimization and learning algorithms, including vector and matrix calculus. Finally, the probability and statistics section covers random variables, point estimation, maximum likelihood estimation, and confidence intervals.\nOn completing this course, students will be well-prepared for a university-level machine learning course that tackles concepts such as gradient descent, neural networks, backpropagation, support vector machines, naive Bayes classifiers, and Gaussian mixture models." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1009 "The course begins with foundational topics in set theory and logic. Students develop a precise mathematical language for describing sets, functions, and predicates, and are introduced to key ideas such as supremum and infimum, as well as argmax and argmin notation. Logical reasoning and Boolean functions are also studied, providing important conceptual motivation for understanding the expressive limitations of some simple machine learning models, such as why certain functions (e.g. XOR) cannot be represented by a single-layer neural network.\nStudents then explore matrices in-depth. They study Gaussian elimination, solve systems of equations, learn about determinants and their properties, and compute inverse matrices. These tools form the computational backbone of many machine learning algorithms.\nAs part of this course, students perform a deep dive into vector spaces, exploring linear independence, subspaces, bases, dimension, rank, and nullity. They also study operations such as the Hadamard product, which appears naturally in element-wise computations in neural networks. Students generalize key concepts to abstract vector spaces and inner product spaces, and study orthogonality, projections, and the Gram–Schmidt process.\nStudents will learn how to find eigenvectors of a matrix, compute matrix diagonalizations, and extend these ideas to orthogonal diagonalization of symmetric matrices. These techniques underpin dimensionality reduction methods and are central to understanding the structure of data.\nThe course then develops key applications of linear algebra in machine learning. Students study singular value decomposition and its relationship to principal component analysis, and learn how these tools are used for dimensionality reduction, feature extraction, and identifying structure in high-dimensional datasets. Linear least-squares problems and regression techniques are also studied as foundational methods for fitting models to data.\nA solid grasp of multivariable calculus is essential for understanding modern machine learning algorithms. In this course, students become well-versed in partial derivatives and gradient vectors (used in gradient-based optimization), the multivariable chain rule (essential for backpropagation), and vector-valued functions. Students extend differentiation to maps between multi-dimensional vector spaces and develop the tools of vector and matrix calculus used to compute gradients of loss functions in neural networks.\nStudents also study optimization of multivariable functions, including critical points, second derivative tests, and constrained optimization using Lagrange multipliers. These techniques form the mathematical foundation of algorithms such as support vector machines and constrained optimization problems in machine learning.\nStudents work with standard multivariable surfaces to build intuition for loss surfaces, and study double integrals as a tool for understanding continuous probability distributions.\nOn the probability and statistics side, students study discrete and continuous random variables, including probability density functions, cumulative distribution functions, transformations, expectation, moments, variance, and Bayes' theorem. Important probability distributions are explored in detail.\nStudents extend their understanding to joint, marginal, and conditional distributions, as well as sums and products of random variables. Topics such as covariance, correlation, and multivariate normal distributions are introduced, which are essential in probabilistic modeling.\nThe course concludes with parametric inference, focusing on point estimation, maximum likelihood estimation, and confidence intervals, which form the statistical foundation for many machine learning methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1012 145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1013 "MML" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046658 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "1ee987ec-c9ff-5d2f-91f4-4de8079c0eac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048955 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048955 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186203999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048955 #edb/ref 1031 "Confidence Intervals for Two Proportions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048955 #edb/ref 1033 4943 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048955 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "cefd0c2d-8f18-5cf5-b1a4-a35b9aa96047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049862 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049862 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049862 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049862 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049862 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049862 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049862 #edb/ref 1031 "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049862 #edb/ref 1033 3932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049862 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "195e726e-f2f5-5d55-938d-79a693a5777a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050186 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050186 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050186 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050186 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050186 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050186 #edb/ref 1031 "Estimating Sample Sizes for Proportions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050186 #edb/ref 1033 3320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050186 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "34cce534-9abb-58ea-ad6e-cdd80c040504" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050241 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050241 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050241 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050241 #edb/ref 1031 "Confidence Intervals for One Proportion: Finite Population Corrections" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050241 #edb/ref 1033 3931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050241 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "38cfa03b-b57f-5a4e-af54-5c91216e5738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1031 "Confidence Intervals for One Mean: Known Population Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1033 260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050921 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "71d6e8f7-677c-5db5-8a47-c2151c322714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050936 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050936 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050936 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050936 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050936 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186171936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050936 #edb/ref 1031 "Confidence Intervals for One Proportion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050936 #edb/ref 1033 3300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050936 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "72cbcd7e-0332-5490-aae5-4381fdc60e68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168807 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168807 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168807 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168807 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186505270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168807 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb8692a1-74f0-5ff4-a1e6-f0adf87799e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168813 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168813 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168813 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168813 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186505510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168813 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8362fec0-95c3-51e3-8cbd-6c9d9645d33c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171681 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171681 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171681 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59510c92-d9be-57c4-bc6d-497107401785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171681 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $n=100$ and $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64,$ we have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} = 100 \\cdot 0.64 = 64 > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 100 \\cdot (1-0.64) = 36 > 5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{|}} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.64\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.64(1-0.64)}{100}} \\\\  & 0.64\\pm 0.079\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171682 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171682 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey of a random sample of $1\\,500$ U.S. households reveals that $780$ of them own at least one dog. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one dog.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p.$* \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171682 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "221d0cad-0cb8-5cdc-816e-19408a796d05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171682 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one dog, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\, 500,$ and\n\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one dog can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,} = \\dfrac{780}{1\\, 500} = 0.52\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} =1\\,500 \\cdot 0.52 = 780 > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 1\\,500 \\cdot (1-0.52) = 720 > 5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{|}} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z > z_{0.025}) = 0.025,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that $z_{0.025}=1.96{:}$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.025}$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.960}$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.52\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.52(1-0.52)}{1\\,500}} \\\\  & 0.52\\pm 0.025\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171935 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "02674ca9-e650-5dc8-93a5-893040bf5342" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171936 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171936 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186172338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171936 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505736 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171936 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171936 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171936 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186505836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171936 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Confidence Intervals for Population Proportions In Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171936 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186171682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186171936 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f2b987ad-6854-59a6-bb39-c7f3c0a3db75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186172726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1045 "Confidence Intervals for One Proportion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186172722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172149 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5ba01719-f8b9-5679-8758-86e56046fcdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172324 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172324 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172324 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172324 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $65%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172324 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b09786c-003f-565a-9fd2-fe6264490f01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172324 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172324 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $n=100$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=65%=0.65,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=100\\cdot 0.65=65>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=100\\cdot (1-0.65)=35>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\n![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.65\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.65(1-0.65)}{100}} \\\\  & 0.65\\pm 0.093\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172325 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172325 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172325 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey of $150$ randomly selected students at a particular university showed that only $24$ select at least one math course. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the proportion of students from the entire university who select at least one math course. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42e7a19f-f4f3-5e6a-9979-741a2870cc54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172325 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172325 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of the students at a particular university,\n\n- some of the students select at least one math course, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=150,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who select at least one math course can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{24}{150}=0.16\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=150\\cdot 0.16=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=150\\cdot (1-0.16)=126>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.16\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.16(1-0.16)}{150}} \\\\  & 0.16\\pm 0.059\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172722 #edb/ref 1061 176376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "89f797af-a45c-58a5-8bec-5490a8e51df2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172723 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172723 #edb/ref 1061 176377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "413a1302-d708-5a68-8969-eee6f8bf3793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172724 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172724 #edb/ref 1061 176378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172724 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d86369dd-0073-526c-baf8-4f7a189e8246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172724 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172725 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172725 #edb/ref 1061 176379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2700b310-2c62-5ffd-ba65-f8b258d201e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172726 #edb/ref 1061 176382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "822975a7-7bca-5cc6-9812-46e5051e550a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173212 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now discuss how to construct a confidence interval for the proportion of the population whose members have a particular characteristic. \n\nAs a concrete example, let $p$ represent the proportion of the United States voting population that will vote for candidate $A$ in the next presidential election. \n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=40$ from our population. After processing our results, we find that the proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ of sample members that plan to vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 0.3.\n$$\n\nThis is our *estimate* of the population proportion $p.$ \n\nOf course, the quantity that we're *really* interested in is $p,$ the proportion of the entire voting population that will vote for candidate $A.$ We want to get an idea of how accurate our estimate is. Therefore, we wish to construct a confidence interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\widehat{\\,p}-E, \\: \\widehat{\\,p}+E),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $E$ is some **margin of error.**\n\nLet's now discuss how to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173212 #edb/ref 1065 6254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173212 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b64af41f-7266-5dc3-9360-aa6c591384e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173212 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173213 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population with sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5$\n\n- $n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5$\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\n\\widehat{\\,p} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\:\n\n\\widehat{\\,p} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are the **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion\n\n- $n$ is the sample size\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/d0/d07c13f30dc4a0960b70f2705bb615f79c0c7ef3a6ed5d7a8eef2401db02d6c2.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{^}{\\,p}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{^}{\\,p}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{^}{\\,p}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}}, & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably.\n\nFinally, in the future, you may assume that if a population is \"large,\" it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173213 #edb/ref 1065 6255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173213 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe719d03-f27c-50c1-8c5d-9bf9e9326e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173213 #edb/ref 1067 "Confidence Intervals for Proportions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173223 #edb/ref 1064 "We have ![](images/4a/4a616e999005a19046f642d63f02ed9d7054edec758f6a03edb7eb90c29e8713.svg) and we wish to construct a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for the population proportion ![](images/1d/1d3e45623fb292952d8c207690797505cc7c484a6410f4a95aff94a51b4a0ee5.svg)\n\nTo construct our confidence interval, the first thing to do is to establish the sampling distribution of ![](images/be/be3d0efacea6b66b85bae982c4d923f2473fe722d5f30c4b9a940ac67773cff2.svg)\n\nSince the United States has around ![](images/59/592c5e42c05234443b91e6d53afea4f21fb7e5dfb0ab1e645fce7ffe881bfc9c.svg) million registered voters, the sample size ![](images/35/3597ee702449dc99d1b986728db87df4b1e6ee3f2ff01bf7db08ad6c48a9bd42.svg) is clearly less than ![](images/41/414c7a992cb07b0cf60bef2446dc3eb4072aeb99acedf6d77c02b5c183cdfc63.svg) of the population size. This means that, provided certain conditions are met, the following approximation can be applied:\n\n![](images/24/24bc598b02901990c48811d40a22934c7f4d59be5e57bcb7f8ac6ca592d1abcb.svg)\n\nLet's check that our usual conditions hold in this case (where we use ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) as an estimate for ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg)):\n\n![](images/bf/bfd75db155b00910cb00aa4c37df692c254df765ec94ba265af589f72868071d.svg)\n\nSince all three conditions hold, the above approximation is applicable. \n\nBy transforming ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n![](images/c4/c461c3f9d172553d6893ddd8bb9f5f9490ba9b3135de8093a367d5286e306344.svg)\n\nSince the true population proportion ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) is unknown, we substitute in this formula the sample proportion ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) for ![](images/f5/f5d3e590cf0347a598e50f45f2e35ce219f3fd9589dfb69628aeb1ba0fdfa60c.svg)\n\n![](images/e7/e7867d67e36fcd33810b482ab26bdfe4be2da4549463e8f1602c65e5a3ae78a0.svg)\n\nWe wish to find a ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)-interval that we're ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confident that the random variable ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\n  \n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter ![](images/b2/b2382453bd24fb0facae9b8be51cbdb4f3606603ab108670fde4f7815fb5d7a4.svg) Since we're computing a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval, we have \n\n![](images/7e/7e6fa76dc615bf00f8cbdf957c653ba7b6217fda2dab0937b1e0cc0741cb7b61.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/28/28f62d4b860fec1ef5c6a7016529d78c308813779a7f12147e508bce162315a7.svg) is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as ![](images/42/420634552d23ada1156e47b7a39985de21735a4d652674e15a481ef25cbf1c37.svg)\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n![](images/48/4875e8608b48eb4fd92e20e26114baf19acb4257cc077d08581483181274d3cc.svg)  \n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that ![](images/74/7437fff22f0f020a39b79d5cb1470b545ba00b61f271a4ac9ee2778c7a549fe2.svg) Therefore,\n\n![](images/b9/b9ce27b3145e86201f04307c54d83681d016c11a60bd04a8159ca81b202c2bd1.svg)\n\nIn other words,\n\n![](images/c9/c9a4066e2b0ed64d8e64b524342b2c875a7bdc62f2eede2c4fb4a96354498ee4.svg)  \n\nTherefore, there is a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) probability that our random variable ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) will lie in the interval ![](images/b6/b62822fdda83ec83c86e736d28f8977e169d277b0a845bef6422a8feed2500d7.svg)\n\nNow, here's the trick. We solve the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population proportion ![](images/f5/f5d3e590cf0347a598e50f45f2e35ce219f3fd9589dfb69628aeb1ba0fdfa60c.svg)\n\n![](images/06/06861abae3aa843df157b835d2505f1e11b1b72108e482a492b98374a395cf14.svg)   \n\nTherefore, a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for the population proportion ![](images/03/0341b1d7529f66db72d7384047a23e81defd5fa012807825ced8393f6f7f9826.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/7e/7ea325842297236a6ed3c3704eb8fa3b6d3c4214d14fc6548c83a63e9d3e2f50.svg)\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n![](images/50/5044d5c3f2e8014d52f939897815813472d6010fe5b9f0903ca9e1fa2692420a.svg)\n\nwe obtain that our ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for ![](images/03/0341b1d7529f66db72d7384047a23e81defd5fa012807825ced8393f6f7f9826.svg) is \n\n![](images/95/95d52028e8b20a7ad509479ea958f31b698f3b74b8c24088ed68dd88bd3d0c90.svg) \n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n![](images/03/03e447d7a11205c8684e92a64b58d7db4e9f8de019c6f99af395211b46e42fdf.svg)\n\nNow that we've seen how to construct a particular confidence interval, let's discuss the more general procedure." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173223 #edb/ref 1065 6462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173223 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "462ecbef-51e0-5a01-808e-cd71bd17cbc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173223 #edb/ref 1067 "Constructing Confidence Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505106 #edb/ref 1080 "0.52\\pm 0.380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505106 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505106 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00ecc0a7-c18b-5d04-8b82-55c9c806c090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505112 #edb/ref 1080 "0.3\\pm 0.051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505112 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505112 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03d4a80f-7422-50eb-a2d2-982d8ab608b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505125 #edb/ref 1080 "0.85\\pm 0.059" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505125 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505125 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a888794-f66d-5afe-bc58-23ba40e1a714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505127 #edb/ref 1080 "0.62\\pm 0.053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505127 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505127 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d110045-4b65-57fd-8bdd-f29c56b9354b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505140 #edb/ref 1080 "0.7\\pm 0.014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505140 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505140 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1396af2f-0e9b-507e-82c5-413a18a2648c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505146 #edb/ref 1080 "0.63\\pm 0.043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505146 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505146 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16c22bf4-1ccd-5590-a353-2f8aba595ef8" #edb