 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a9ab8b3f-5403-5ce1-916c-6c3e8a70acc6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505710 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186505594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505710 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505710 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505710 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186505594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505710 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d5a0c197-f408-509a-9c1f-a1eeb5f17467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505736 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505736 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505736 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505736 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey of $1\\,200$ randomly selected U.S. households reveals that $816$ of them own at least one pet. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one pet.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505736 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "139a4b78-66bf-579e-be6b-e3d4e7bd7403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505736 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one pet, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\,200,$ and\n\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one pet can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{816}{1\\,200}=0.68\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=1\\,200\\cdot 0.68=816>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=1\\,200\\cdot (1-0.68)=384>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,68\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.68(1-0.68)}{1\\,200}} \\\\  & 0.68\\pm 0.035\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505767 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505767 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505767 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505767 #edb/ref 1071 "The financial manager of a large company conducts a random sample of $120$ accounts receivable and finds that $42$ of them are more than $30$ days past due. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all of the company's accounts receivable that are more than $30$ days past due. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505767 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55f340e1-5420-5652-bd4b-d4b5bdb68c7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505767 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all accounts receivable from a certain company,\n\n- some of the accounts selected are more than $30$ days past due, and some are not,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=120,$ and\n\n- the proportion of accounts receivable in the sample that are more than $30$ days past due can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{42}{120}=0.35\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.35=42>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.35)=78>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.35\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.35(1-0.35)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.35\\pm 0.112\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505771 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505771 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143307" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505771 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505771 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey shows that $66$ out of a random sample of $220$ fans of a popular MLB baseball team are against the way their current coach has run the team.\nFind a $90%$ confidence interval for the proportion of the team's fans who are against the way the coach has run the team.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505771 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b158e8c-e5d9-51ed-a647-72626d39b20a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505771 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of fans of a certain MLB baseball team,\n\n- some of the fans are against the way their current coach has run the team, and some are not,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=220,$ and\n\n- the proportion of fans from the sample who are against the way the coach has run the team can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{66}{220}=0.3\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=220\\cdot 0.3=66>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=220\\cdot (1-0.3)=154>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2e/2ed94fdbb0eb4cc1dda3720592488546a02e157598e77a70fbd02d916906b56f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{220}} \\\\  & 0.3\\pm 0.051\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505798 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505798 #edb/ref 1069 "q-225614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505798 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505798 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=90$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.6,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505798 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acfb542c-f97d-5ef1-8816-235f54148d5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505798 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $n=90$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.6,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=90\\cdot 0.6=54>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=90\\cdot (1-0.6)=36>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.6\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{90}} \\\\  & 0.6\\pm 0.101\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505802 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505802 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143374" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505802 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505802 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=140$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $15%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505802 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af2f2f44-7a26-5871-833f-ef0bfbcbad94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505802 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $n=140$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=15%=0.15,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=140\\cdot 0.15=21>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=140\\cdot (1-0.15)=119>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.15\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{140}} \\\\  & 0.15\\pm 0.050\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505831 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505831 #edb/ref 1069 "q-225615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505831 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505831 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/68/6867cf1d5e64f785003037162c6e62d0531d6c4dd8330adf139f8ae8ac46082b.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6190115-0559-574a-ba01-3e43de706837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505831 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $n=120$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.4=48>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.4)=72>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.088\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505836 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505836 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505836 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505836 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey of $480$ randomly selected U.S. adults reveals that $336$ of the respondents have read at least one book in the past year. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. adults who read at least one book in the past year.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505836 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec6576c2-76eb-5af7-a883-a898d0a92018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505836 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of  all U.S. adults,\n\n- some of the respondents read at least one book in the past year, and some didn't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=480,$ and\n\n- the proportion of adults who read at least one book in the past year can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{336}{480}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=480\\cdot 0.7=336>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=480\\cdot (1-0.7)=144>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{480}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.041\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505844 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505844 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143371" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505844 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505844 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505844 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd266226-8e47-5093-b6f1-e853e4a5f194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505844 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $n=120$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.2,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=120\\cdot 0.2=24>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=120\\cdot (1-0.2)=96>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.2\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{120}} \\\\  & 0.2\\pm 0.060\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
